# Review: ConductorPlugin

The plugin itself is thin: it contributes a create-object entry, the schemas and one surface
(`CanvasArticle`). The behaviour lives in `@dxos/conductor` (types, node kernels, the fiber executor) and
`@dxos/react-ui-canvas-compute` (shapes, the scene projection and `ComputeGraphController`). The types,
the pull-based executor, `ValueBag`/`NotExecuted` semantics, the `if` router and the canvas
editing round-trip are solid and tested (`fiber-compiler.test.ts`, `scene.test.ts`, the playwright
`scene.spec.ts`). The data and AI nodes are the weak part: `database` and `text-to-image` throw
`Not implemented`, `thread` and `reducer` have no kernel, the feed branch of `append` is dead code,
and the GPT node ignores its `model` and `tools` inputs. The biggest divergence is `invokeGpt`, which
takes a `prompt`/`history`/`conversation` bag and returns messages, text and a token stream instead of
reading a `Ref<Thread>` and returning one `Message`. `CanvasArticle` has moved to the scene engine, so
its declared state no longer matches. None of the declared op errors (`MissingValue`,
`InvalidExpression`, `ModelUnavailable`, ...) exist as typed errors; failures are `invariant`s, thrown
`Error`s or `orDie`.

## Extensions

